for every acclaimed action movie there  are a dozen more that were shredded by  critics can fairness a lot of those  films probably deserve their lousy  rotten tomato scores after all making an  action movie is no easy task and you  need more than a couple of explosions to  create an awesome film on the other hand  Rotten Tomatoes is a website that lacks  nuance and sometimes you might find an  adrenaline laced gem under all those  slimy green splotches from films with  monstrous snakes to gigantic gods these  action movies might have terrible tomato  meters but they're still worth watching  plagued by production woes and a  whitewashing controversy The Lone Ranger  was in trouble from day one and granted  Gore Verbinski's film is a mess but in a  way that's what makes it so fascinating  there are so many ideas at play here  from the exploitation of Chinese  laborers and the genocide of Native  Americans to the takeover of American  politics by corrupt corporations the  cinematography is sweeping and beautiful  and some of the sets feel like they were  taken from a baz lurman movie while the  decision to cast Johnny Depp as Tonto  may have been culturally insensitive he  is actually hilarious and he has real  chemistry with the charming army hammer  if you could just point us in the right  direction we'll take it from here what's  that nothing while it's no Pirates of  the Caribbean  The Lone Ranger doesn't deserve its bad  reputation and proves even the messiest  movies have a silver lining  set in the Amazon jungle anaconda is a  big dumb monster flick that traffic's  and b-movie scares and we mean that in  the best possible way it follows a group  of documentary filmmakers led by  Jennifer Lopez who run into the world's  biggest and hungriest snake the creature  Yanks its victims underwater snatches  them out of the air and never seems to  get its fill of human flesh and while  it's occasionally done with silly CG  there's also a fantastic animatronic  snake that looks like it escaped from an  evil rainforest cafe but the serpent  isn't even the highlight of the movie  the real draw is Jon Voight as a smarmy  poacher with an untraceable accent he'll  ears and sneers every second he's on  camera all the while plotting ways to  catch the killer reptile even if that  means using human bait you should just  ignore the cynical critics  and anaconda because they're just trying  to squeeze all the fun out of the cinema  2006 is Tokyo Drift is the black sheep  of the Fast and Furious franchise Paul  Walker is nowhere to be found  Vin Diesel only shows up for a few  seconds and the undercover cops and  international heists are replaced by a  soapy teenage melodrama the plot follows  a troubled American kid named Sean who  moves to Japan gets involved with the  Yakuza and is forced to race the local  bully to save his buddies his girlfriend  and his own honor of course before he  can become the king of the road this  hotshot has to learn how to drift in  other words it's the Karate Kid with  cars  [Music]  watching these hot rods drift their way  down mountain roads is pretty impressive  and even kind of beautiful on top of all  that Tokyo Drift introduces some key  players to the F&amp;F franchise actors sun  kang director justin lin and writer  chris morgan these guys would be  instrumental in shaping the series going  forward but even as its own movie Tokyo  Drift is a slick looking film with fun  races and characters we care about  guy ritchie is best known for making  comedic crime thrillers which would seem  to make him a weird pick to direct King  Arthur legend of the sword and  admittedly there are parts of this film  that are a chore to sift through but you  can't deny its ambition especially when  the monsters roll onto the screen there  are war elephants to tower over castles  and then we watch as a massive writhing  tentacles reveal slimy witches that are  part woman part Kraken we kept outside  of the Covenant it was you lost the boy  and the soul sacrifice too no but  there's more going on here than just  high fantasy Richie brings his trademark  humor to the film and almost every  character acts like a cockney criminal  making King Arthur legend of the sword a  wonderfully weird way to spend a  Saturday afternoon  tell me story about a goko Lucy a Viking  called grey bid of some rebel graffiti  duty in these worlds are you writing a  book  critics hated bright in fact they  loathed it and you know what it's  totally understandable bright has a lot  of problems from its lazy world-building  and sloppy screenplay to it's awkward  handling of racial issues but while it  doesn't live up to its potential it's  nevertheless filled with striking images  like a cent or cop and a dragon flying  over Los Angeles and while the story  mechanics and character arcs are messy  David Ayer knows how to direct an action  scene the gas station shootout is a  highlight and the moment when Will Smith  saves his orc partner from a gang of  murderous cops is wonderfully done but  the best thing about bright has got to  be Joel Edgerton who plays the first orc  ever hired to work for the LAPD he  wonderfully conveys pain underneath all  his prosthetics and you should check out  bright for his performance alone he  fulfills a great prophecy would I think  we might be in a prophecy Universal  Studios has been trying to create a  monstrous cinematic universe for quite  some time before The Mummy completely  unraveled in 2017 the studio was hoping  to get its dark universe going with  Dracula untold in 2014 unfortunately  lousy box office numbers and a poor  tomato meter forced the studio to stake  this film in the heart but while it's  not a great movie by any stretch of the  imagination Dracula untold is  wonderfully dumb fun it's basically a  vampire movie meets a superhero flick  directed by Gary Shore Dracula untold  finds Luke Evans as the titular  bloodsucker only instead of trying to  murder innocent women  this Dracula is an undead superhero  trying to protect his homeland from  invading Turks this dark tale truly  shines when Dracula is beating up bad  guys he plows into a platoon of goons  like a mad NFL player  all while roaring like a lion and using  force like powers he commands a legion  of bats to batter his enemies he even  creates his own vampire army resulting  in a brilliantly bloody climax this  isn't high art but if you've ever  wondered what would happen if Dracula  used his bat powers for good then this  is the movie for you  Boyko sick isn't it save me yes and I  would do it all again directed by James  Wan death sentence says the gore of saw  the scares of the conjuring and the  family feels of Furious 7  in fact we're legitimately shocked that  it has such a low tomato meter unlike  most of the films on this list there are  no obvious reasons why it was blasted so  hard by critics instead this 2007  revenge thriller is gritty gross and  utterly compelling  with its opening montage of home video  footage we're rooting for Nik Hume from  frame 1 he's a good dad and a loving  husband so naturally he's devastated  when his oldest son is murdered by a  machete-wielding gangster when Nik  realizes the legal system is going to  let the killer off easy  he takes justice into his own hands but  what starts off as a simple act of  revenge spirals into full-on war when  the criminals brother comes back for  blood Kevin Bacon is the key to the  movie's success he's not some Charles  Bronson super vigilante he's a real  human being struggling with guilt and  grief he barely knows how to use a gun  and he breaks down into tears after  murdering his son's killer you really  feel the love this guy has for his  family and his hatred for the thugs who  threatened his home in addition to bacon  there's John Goodman as an F bomb  dropping force of nature who Selznick  his cache of weapons in the best gun  buying scene since  taxi driver dad - dad don't tell me  about it just go do it  it all caps off with one of cinema's  most underrated gunfights an epic battle  awash in red light death sentence is a  badass bloody affair that proves Rotten  Tomatoes can sometimes get it wrong  2016's gods of Egypt was lambasted for  its Eurocentric cast cheesy acting and  shabby CGI but it's over-the-top  performances and bizarre look should  actually make it worth your while said  in ancient Egypt the plot follows a  human thief who's caught a struggle  between two larger-than-life deities  this mythological conflict takes our  characters into outer space before  plunging them into the underworld while  the movie is absolutely bonkers when it  comes to the visuals it also somehow  still plays everything perfectly  straight there's no cynical winking or  annoying irony so if you enjoy big  hearted adventures then gods of Egypt  will be good for your soul  it's easy to see why Hansel and Gretel  witch hunters has such a low tomato  meter it's mostly because of Hansel and  Gretel  they don't have character arcs their  dialogue is flat and there's nothing  distinguishing them from one another  besides the fact that one is played by  Jeremy Renner and the other by Gemma  Arterton really they're just two  badasses who run around and blow up  witches yeah yeah yeah  how do you do - Nathan candy but if  you're into seeing evil creatures  getting their heads cut off  then Hansel and Gretel witch hunters is  the movie for you this 2013 horror  adventure is like army of Darkness on  crack  complete with anachronistic weapons  buckets of blood and more wicked old  hags than you can shake a broomstick in  the monster makeup is on point and the  flying effects are both creepy and cool  and while Renner and Arterton aren't  doing a lot with their characters femke  Johnson more than makes up for it as the  villainous head witch geez big she's bad  she's over-the-top glorious and Peter  Stormare plays a sheriff bringing the  smarm for everyone to hate granted  watching Renner punch women in the face  is a bit unsettling but for the most  part The Kills here are creative if  you're in the mood for a movie that's  lost its mind this witchy flop is for  you  [Music]
 

